1461:Ladies and gentlemen, this finalist is about to present a coffee crafted using a unique technique passed down from her parents –
1462:The air tastes of new spring and fresh coffee.//nSitting in the audience, I keep my gaze fixed on the girl under the spotlight.
1463:And there is Reina, gently polishing her gooseneck kettle.//nHer long lashes catch the light, casting tiny shadows. The noise of the crowd, the weight of this moment... none of it seems to reach her.
1464:Nobody else knows what she went through – all the obstacles she overcame just to stand here today.
1465:Two months ago.
1466:With the truth of the accident finally laid bare, Reina made two decisions.//nThe first was to restart the physical therapy she'd once abandoned.
1467:Miss Nanami, I have to say, you really surprised me. You came in on your own and asked for a full checkup. There's something different in your eyes. I'm so happy for you.
1468:Umm... thank you, Doctor. Things have... really changed lately.
1469:So, what I really came for... is to know the truth. The real truth.
1470:My legs... Is it still possible for me to stand?//nWhatever you tell me, I'll be okay with it.
1471:Relax. Just like all your previous checkups, your nerves and muscles are at normal levels.//nPhysically, there's no reason you can't stand. You just need to train and break through that mental barrier.
1472:Mental barrier?
1473:Yes. Subconsciously, you've grown reliant on the wheelchair. It's become your safety net. In my experience, what breaks that cycle is almost always a sudden, powerful emotional trigger.
1474:In other words, you need a trigger so intense that your desire to stand overpowers your fear. And to be perfectly honest, experiencing a moment like that takes a bit of luck.
1475:We can't just wait for that to happen, though.//nStarting now, consistent physical rehab is how you'll build up every bit of strength you can, so when that moment arrives, you'll be ready.
1476:I understand.
1477:Thank you, Doctor. I'll push through every bit of it, no matter how hard it gets.
1478:And her second decision? To enter the City Brewers Cup.
1479:Oh! Aren't you the manager of Éternité? You're competing in this too?!
1480:Sorry, I got a bit excited! I'm a regular at your place, actually. Your coffee is incredible.
1481:Oh, that's so kind of you!
1482:However... compared to the other competitors, you might be at a bit of a disadvantage given your circumstances... Are you sure about this?
1483:Umm... I suppose I really am at a disadvantage.
1486:Does competing in a wheelchair make a difference?
1487:Oh, because making pour-over coffee needs really steady hands when you're pouring.
1488:Usually, when standing, you use your entire core to keep the water flow steady. But sitting down... you're relying entirely on arm strength, which makes it much harder to keep the kettle from shaking. If your pour isn't stable, the coffee suffers.
1489:Exactly... the fact that you can make coffee that good while seated is already remarkable. But to go up against standing baristas...
1490:I know.
1491:But... if I let the odds stop me from even trying, then I haven't really changed at all, have I?
1492:The harder it is, the more every little success will mean to me. That's exactly why I need to compete.
1493:I'm sure about this. Sign me up!
1494:They kept telling her she needed luck, that her chances were slim... but none of it ever seemed to faze her.
1495:Instead, she threw herself into a grueling training routine.
1496:Aren't you pushing yourself too hard with both the competition and rehab?
1497:We don't have to rush this, you know.
1498:Reina was focused on her pour-over. She paused for a tiny moment when she heard me, but kept going.
1499:The stream held steady as it swirled into the dripper, filling the room with a deep, rich aroma. When the pour was complete, she finally set the gooseneck kettle down gently.
1500:{var:player_nickname}, thanks for worrying about me.
1501:The old me would have already run off to cry somewhere by now.
1502:But I'm not her anymore.
1503:Let me show you something.
1504:From the very back of a drawer behind the counter, she pulled out an elegant card and handed it to me.
1505:It was a greeting card with pretty handwriting that had faded a bit with time.
1506:..."May you find eternal happiness, Reina."//nIs this... from your parents?
1507:Yeah. It was the year Éternité opened. I was four years old, and they wrote this for me.
1508:When you helped me find the truth... I could finally bring myself to open up their things. This card was waiting in a photo album.
1509:"Éternité"... eternity.
1510:All they wanted was for you to be happy.
1511:Mm. Being at Éternité all these years... I think I finally understand the meaning behind the name.
1512:My parents' wish was for me to always be happy. But {var:player_nickname}... you're the one who gave me the courage to love again, and to let myself be loved.
1513:I won't let this wish for "eternity" go unanswered... and I won't let you down either. Not anymore.
1514:I want to stand. I want to compete. And that's not just for them.
1515:It's for me, too. I want a new start, one that doesn't have to carry the past.
1516:As for whether doing both of these at the same time is too much...
1517:Making coffee is what I love most, anyway! There's really no better way to shake off the stress of rehab.//nBesides, building up my core in physical therapy is only going to make my pours even steadier.
1518:So yeah... it's going to take a lot more than this to keep me down!
1519:She gave me a bright, shining smile, full of nothing but determination.
1520:{var:player_nickname}, will you stay with me through all of this?
1521:Of course. I'll be right here.
1522:Thank you!
1523:Then let's do this!
