1633:(I rushed too fast, slipped like an idiot. At least nothing's broken.)
1634:(But I got it, her certificate. It's okay now.)
1635:What I see next is something I'll never forget for the rest of my life.
1636:Reina's thin, frail body wavers as if she'd collapse any second, yet she keeps moving toward me, one stubborn step at a time.
1637:She looks up and meets my eyes.
1638:Reina!
1639:Right before she collapses, I lunge forward and catch her, wrapping my arms around her as tightly as I can.
1640:Oh, thank god.
1641:Yeah, I'm okay. I'm right here.
1642:I can feel her trembling in my arms, and beneath that tremor, her legs straining, trying to support just a little of her own weight.
1643:Reina... look. You're standing right now.
1644:Uhh... I-I'm standing...?
1645:The rain stops without either of us noticing.
1646:Sunlight breaks through the heavy clouds, bathing the world in a soft, golden glow as a rainbow arches across the sky.
1647:After catching our breath, I steadily help Reina walk the rest of the way to her parents' grave.
1648:With great care, she places the certificate – a little wet, but perfectly intact – in front of the headstone.
1649:Dad... Mom... It's Reina. I got my first barista award! Pretty cool, right?
1650:And... this is {var:player_nickname}.
1651:I'm doing well now... and I'm really happy. So please, you don't have to worry about me anymore.
1652:Seemingly out of nowhere, a beautiful butterfly lands softly on the headstone. It rests for a moment before fluttering up into the bright, clear sky.
1653:She laughs, even as the tears still shine in her eyes.
1654:...I hear you. Thank you for your blessing, Dad, Mom.
1655:As we leave, her walking is much steadier with my support.//nShe still leans on me, but she's taking every step on her own.
1656:Reina, remember that surprise I told you about?
1657:I take the gift I'd prepared out of my bag.
1658:It's the flower spirit painting, but remade. The things that had trapped the flower spirit are now gone, and the spirit is finally... smiling.
1659:This is a new portrait for you. The real you.
1660:Reina, you can fly anywhere you want now, on your own wings.
1661:And I'll always be right there beside you.
1662:Reina doesn't say anything. Looking deep into my eyes, she gives a warm smile and gently holds out her hand.
1663:Together, you and me!
1664:Reina, I think it went all the way down to the bottom of the slope. I can't see where it landed from here.
1665:No, don't. Really, it's just... a piece of paper. Mom and Dad will still hear me without it.
1666:I'd much rather you stay safe than get hurt over this...
1667:Though she says that, I can clearly see the disappointment in Reina's face.
1668:It's okay, I'll be careful.
1669:It might take a while. Wait here for me.
1670:I reassure her before finding a safer path down. A short search later, I finally spot the certificate.
1671:...Thank you, {var:player_nickname}.
1672:Her tight grip on the wheelchair finally relaxes.
1673:I step back, giving her space for a quiet moment with her parents.
1674:On the way home, she's quieter than usual, but her eyes are more determined than ever.
1675:{var:player_nickname}, I've made up my mind. Starting tomorrow, I'm pushing myself twice as hard in rehab.
1676:Not just so you won't have to take risks for me... it's for me, too.
1677:I don't want to live with any more "what-ifs." I want to stand beside the people I care about, on my own two feet.
1678:And when I do... you'll be the first to know!
1679:I know you will. I'll be waiting.
1680:Her legs have yet to heal, but her spirit has already taken flight toward tomorrow.
1683:I can't believe how much you've improved in just a week. You're seriously talented.
